## Best Practices

### 1. Authentication

✓ **DO:**
- Use HTTPS for all API calls
- Store tokens securely (in secure HTTP-only cookies for web)
- Refresh tokens before expiry
- Implement token rotation
- Log out users explicitly
- Monitor for suspicious authentication patterns

✗ **DON'T:**
- Send tokens in URL parameters
- Store tokens in local storage (use secure cookies)
- Use hardcoded tokens
- Expose tokens in logs or error messages
- Use HTTP instead of HTTPS

### 2. Rate Limiting

✓ **DO:**
- Implement client-side rate limit handling
- Use exponential backoff for retries
- Check `Retry-After` header for wait duration
- Monitor your API usage
- Implement circuit breaker pattern for production

✗ **DON'T:**
- Ignore 429 responses
- Retry immediately without delay
- Overwhelm API with burst requests
- Bypass rate limits with multiple API keys
- Log in repeatedly to reset rate limits

### 3. Webhook Security

✓ **DO:**
- Always verify webhook signatures before processing
- Use the raw request body for signature verification
- Implement idempotent webhook handlers
- Log all webhook events for audit
- Return 2xx response after processing
- Implement exponential backoff for retries

✗ **DON'T:**
- Process webhooks without signature verification
- Use parsed/serialized body for signature validation
- Process duplicate webhook events
- Ignore signature mismatches
- Store sensitive data in webhook logs
- Process webhooks synchronously for long operations

### 4. Error Handling

✓ **DO:**
- Log all errors with context
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Use appropriate HTTP status codes
- Include trace IDs for debugging
- Implement proper error recovery
- Validate responses before use

✗ **DON'T:**
- Expose internal error details to clients
- Return 200 for errors
- Ignore errors silently
- Retry on client errors (4xx)
- Log sensitive data in errors

### 5. API Versioning

✓ **DO:**
- Use semantic versioning
- Maintain backward compatibility
- Deprecate features with warning period
- Document breaking changes
- Test version migrations

✗ **DON'T:**
- Remove endpoints without deprecation
- Change response formats without notice
- Mix API versions in single request

### 6. Performance

✓ **DO:**
- Implement pagination for list endpoints
- Use filtering and searching efficiently
- Cache appropriate responses
- Compress responses
- Monitor API performance

✗ **DON'T:**
- Return unlimited results
- Perform expensive operations
- Make multiple API calls when one would suffice
- Ignore pagination

### 7. Data Protection

✓ **DO:**
- Use HTTPS for all communications
- Encrypt sensitive data at rest
- Implement data retention policies
- Audit data access
- Use encryption keys from Azure Key Vault

✗ **DON'T:**
- Send sensitive data in URL parameters
- Store passwords in plaintext
- Log sensitive information
- Use hardcoded encryption keys
- Expose customer data in error messages

### 8. API Documentation

✓ **DO:**
- Keep documentation up-to-date
- Provide code examples
- Document all parameters and responses
- Include error scenarios
- Explain authentication requirements
- Document rate limits

✗ **DON'T:**
- Leave code undocumented
- Document outdated endpoints
- Forget to mention rate limits
- Omit authentication details

